===persian===

و از آن پس...
باران مشتاقی بودم... که...
بر پیکر سروی بی‌تاب می‌بارید...
سرو را می‌شست و...
به امّیدِ بار دیگر... که...
به آسمان برگردد... و... باران شَود...
با بوی سرو به دریا می‌رفت.
آنک صدای دعای زنی‌ست...
«آه ای خورشید روزهای روشن،
از تبارِ پرندگان دریایی...
تندتر بتاب تا موج‌های بی‌قرار...
ابر شوند... و...
بر تمامی کویرهای زمینی...
سبزه برویید...»
و از آن پس...
بارانِ پُر از ترانه‌ای بودم...
که بر پیکرِ بی‌تابِ سروهایِ جهان می‌بارید...
سرو را می‌شست...
و از پای سرو به بستر رود می‌رفت...
رود که... دیوانهٔ دریا بود...
که خوب و زیبا بود...
۱۳۸۱/۹/۷

===machine===

And after that...
I was an eager rain... that...
fell on the body of a restless cypress...
It washed the cypress and...
hoping for one more turn... that...
it might climb back to the sky... and... become rain...
it went down to the sea with the scent of cypress on it.
And there is the sound of a woman praying...
"Ah, sun of the bright days,
of the lineage of the sea-birds...
burn harder, so that the restless waves...
turn to cloud... and...
over every desert of the earth...
green things grow..."
And after that...
I was a rain brim-full of a song...
that fell on the restless body of the cypresses of the world...
It washed the cypress...
and from the cypress's foot went down to the river's bed...
the river that... was mad for the sea...
that was good, and beautiful...
1381/9/7

===lantern===

And after that
I was an eager rain
falling on the body of a restless cypress.
It washed the cypress and,
hoping for one more turn —
that it might climb back to the sky and become rain —
went down to the sea with the scent of cypress on it.
And there is the sound of a woman praying:
"Ah, sun of the bright days,
of the lineage of the sea-birds,
burn harder, so that the restless waves
turn to cloud, and
over every desert of the earth
green things grow..."
And after that
I was a rain brim-full of a song
falling on the restless body of the cypresses of the world.
It washed the cypress
and from the cypress's foot went down to the river's bed —
the river that was mad for the sea,
that was good, and beautiful...

1381/9/7

